Temperature of what, when, and under which thermal conditions? (@met-temperature: 0, is #chapter, #part-of @metrology, #source "../sources/Thermal-Enclosures.lingua#r_4A4JXBQPJ1SXY82FVV367HSN70", #source "../sources/SI-Realizations.lingua#r_240WHEHDVW9TR241AXP6T4STM0", #source "../sources/Uncertainty-NIST-1900.lingua#r_75WCVW8CJG4TAFD3330T87588E", #metrology "../practice/Reference-life-cycle.lingua#r_1ZS7B98GYPWX6S7BFZ5S6CY6YR", #thermodynamics-and-heat-transfer "../../../stem/physics/thermodynamics-and-heat-transfer/measurement/Temperature-response-contact-and-calibration.lingua#r_4R7759MYCC3X3GBCZ5GRSN188W") { r_480AQX951V9NFA6AB86KJA1QZD
**Read:** [Calibration of temperature and humidity enclosures — EURAMET cg-20](../sources/Thermal-Enclosures.lingua#r_4A4JXBQPJ1SXY82FVV367HSN70), the full enclosure guide and the relevant instrument guide; [Practical realizations of SI units — BIPM consultative committees](../sources/SI-Realizations.lingua#r_240WHEHDVW9TR241AXP6T4STM0), the kelvin document; [Simple Guide for Evaluating and Expressing Uncertainty — Possolo](../sources/Uncertainty-NIST-1900.lingua#r_75WCVW8CJG4TAFD3330T87588E), E2 Surface Temperature, E7 Thermistor Calibration, and E20 Thermal Bath.

Distinguish thermodynamic temperature, a specified practical temperature-scale realization, sensor indication, and the temperature of the actual specimen or region. A calibrated sensor in a bath does not automatically characterize a loaded enclosure, a moving surface, or a sample that has not equilibrated.

Important influences include sensor calibration, immersion and stem conduction, contact, self-heating, radiation, spatial gradients, temporal instability, hysteresis, response time, and readout electronics. Different principles expose different combinations. A contact sensor and a radiation thermometer can disagree because they interrogate different interactions, not only because one is defective.

A thermal enclosure’s working volume and loading conditions are part of the intended result. Map spatial and temporal behavior with an appropriate design instead of averaging all readings into a single unexplained number. A reference sensor’s calibration uncertainty is only one contribution to the uncertainty of a sample temperature in that volume.

**Work:** read cg-20 in full and reconstruct the thermal-bath or surface-temperature example in TN 1900. Write a teaching mapping plan that distinguishes sensor offsets, location differences, and time changes. Explain which effects a single fixed sensor cannot identify. Compare an equilibrium study with a first-order transient response and determine when a reading is adequate for a stated purpose.

For advanced study, read the full relevant kelvin realization and one complete instrument-specific thermocouple or block-calibrator guide. Preserve the distinction between the unit’s definition, the practical reference procedure, and the conditions of an industrial temperature result. Calculations may be reconstructed from published data without building a high-temperature facility.

**Continue:** [Produce, preserve, transfer, and replace the references](../practice/Reference-life-cycle.lingua#r_1ZS7B98GYPWX6S7BFZ5S6CY6YR).

